      "a": "Contact with volcanic ash can cause skin inflammation, pain, swelling, and infections from scratches [1]. Volcanic ash is very small and enters the lungs with the air, which can increase coughing, shortness of breath, runny nose, and sore nose or throat. People with asthma or bronchitis may experience attacks like coughing fits, chest tightness, and labored breathing, and those with serious heart conditions should also be cautious [2]. If volcanic ash gets into your eyes, do not rub them; rinse them with water and use glasses instead of contact lenses [2].",
